   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.5. Increased capacity of constituents to develop fair and effective labour migration frameworks, institutions and services to protect migrant workers</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Nigerian Government revised the 2014 National Policy on Labour Migration in July 2021. The revision process was carried out by the Federal Ministry of Labour and Employment in close collaboration with social partners and relevant stakeholders and in line with the institutional framework for policy implementation. The revised National Policy on Labour Migration was validated by members of the Technical Working Committee  in July 2021.
 
The Policy provides a national framework for effective labour migration governance in Nigeria with an accompanying plan of action for implementation. It emphasizes gender sensitivity and responsiveness and promotes a rights-based approach including through sections on the needs and protection of women migrant workers and other vulnerable groups, as well as on grievance and justice mechanisms

In 2021, national technical skills committees in Ghana (Council for Technical and Vocational Education and Training (COTVET), Nigeria (National Board for Technical Education) and Togo produced harmonized occupational minimal standards for plumbing, masonry and agriculture. Tripartite constituents from Nigeria, Ghana and Togo had pledged to strengthen mutual recognition of each other&#x2019;s training certificates and/or harmonize curricula in the agriculture and construction sectors/occupations affected most strongly by migration between the three countries. Five key occupations were selected within each sector, through a participative and inclusive process that involved a tripartite group of national constituents in the three countries. Occupational standards and qualifications in agriculture and construction sectors were then mapped against one another to identify commonalities and differences. These two phases were agreed upon with constituents to ensure the relevance of mutually recognized/harmonized education and training programs in the context of migration between the three countries. (result links to GHA904). With support of the Government of Germany and Switzerland, the ILO provided inputs, comments and feedback to the revision of the 2014 National Policy on Labour Migration. Support was provided to the Federal Ministry of Labour and Employment to facilitate stakeholder consultations including meetings of the Technical Working Committee and the Social Partner Advisory Committee to ensure it validated the revised Policy by members of the Technical Working Committee. The engagement of social partners in the area of labour migration governance was further promoted through dedicated capacity building activities with employers&#x2019; organizations in February 2020 and July 2021 and with worker&#x2019;s organizations in November 2019 and July 2021. Representatives from Government, social partners and other stakeholders benefited from an e-learning training course on labour migration governance and coherence with employment policies that was implemented by the International Training Centre of the ILO and included components targeted specifically for participants from Nigeria in May-June 2020.

With support from the European Union and Norway, the ILO conducted studies in West Africa and the Sahel on the nexus between migration and skills policies, assessing migration trends, priority occupations and how skills development and recognition could benefit migrant workers. In September 2019, tripartite consultations were conducted to validate the findings and identify opportunities to forge skills partnerships. The findings of this study and the identified occupational standards were validated by tripartite representatives of Ghana, Nigeria and Togo during an online workshop on 5th of November 2020. An online consultation process followed with 13 meetings with tripartite representatives of Ghana, Nigeria and Togo by end of 2020. It defined next steps of the process and prioritized the creation of harmonized occupational minimal standards for masonry, plumbing and poultry rearing.</narrative>
   </description>
